stuff we throw away every day is staggering. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: How do you think the situation could be improved?<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">John Smits</span>: Society needs to feel involved in this important matter. After the first wave of environmental treaties were signed 20 years ago, awareness programs were created throughout our country. That's a positive sign, but we need to work tirelessly if we want to see them thrive.", "id": 2, "time": "16000", "image": "engenn7le17ob04re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: Would you say that irreparable damage was done by the previous generation? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">John Smits</span>: Yes, to a certain extent. Climate change wasn't studied by scientists as widely as it is now. People only started to notice once our cause was endorsed by movie stars! <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: So, do you believe that celebrities will save the day, one tweet at a time? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">John Smits</span>: Absolutely. Throngs of people follow them online. They have a duty to speak out for the earth!", "id": 3, "time": "42000", "image": "engenn7le17ob04re2img02.jpg"}]}}]}]}], "type": "sena"}